string runPurchaseInflow()
{
	// THE ONE ENTRY POINT EVERY AUTOMATIC CALLER USES. No arguments, on purpose.
	//
	// syncPurchaseInflow takes a dryRun string, and a webhook URL with that
	// parameter missing or mistyped is a silent failure of the worst kind: it
	// runs as a dry run for ever, lands nothing, and reports success the whole
	// time. A no-argument Custom API cannot be called wrong. syncPurchaseInflow
	// stays directly runnable from Execute, which is the debugging path.
	//
	// Called by:
	//   - the Inventory Purchase Receive workflow webhook, on create or edit
	//   - the store widget's "Check for arrivals" button
	//
	// Neither caller passes anything, and neither needs to: the Inventory_Inflow
	// ledger works out what is new. That is why this can be fired on every
	// receive, as often as you like, without double counting - a document whose
	// last_modified_time has not moved is not even opened.
	//
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// THERE IS NO LOCK HERE, AND THAT IS A DECIDED RISK, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
	//
	// Two runs overlapping would both read Applied_Qty = 0 for the same line,
	// both compute the same delta, and both apply it - crediting cloth twice.
	// It does NOT self-correct: the next run finds the document and the ledger
	// agreeing, so nothing is left to notice it. Only reconcileRawMaterial would
	// ever show the drift.
	//
	// Skipped because the window is the two or three seconds a run takes, and
	// this factory books a handful of purchase receives a week. Two inside the
	// same couple of seconds is not a realistic event at that volume.
	//
	// ADD IT WHEN EITHER OF THESE BECOMES TRUE:
	//   - receives are booked in batches, or by more than one person at once
	//   - a second automatic trigger is wired (a bill rule, a schedule, Flow)
	//
	// The shape it would take is written up in docs/inventory-build-plan.md: a
	// Sync_Lock row taken before the work and released after, with staleness
	// measured as a stored day plus a minute-of-day integer rather than datetime
	// arithmetic, because the one path that cannot release a lock is the
	// statement-execution limit and that is not catchable. It is not atomic
	// either - Deluge has no test-and-set - so it narrows the window rather than
	// closing it. Adding it later touches this function and a new form only, and
	// NEVER the webhook, which is the reason the no-argument wrapper exists
	// separately from the locking question.

	res = "";
	errTxt = "";

	try
	{
		syncRes = "";

		// Its own try/catch so a thrown sync still comes back as a readable
		// reply. Creator collapses every runtime failure into a generic code
		// 9430, and a webhook has nowhere to show that even if it did not.
		try
		{
			syncRes = thisapp.syncPurchaseInflow("false").toString();
		}
		catch (eSync)
		{
			syncRes = "{\"errors\":[\"runPurchaseInflow: " + eSync.toString().replaceAll("\"","'").replaceAll("\r","").replaceAll("\n"," | ") + "\"]}";
		}

		info "runPurchaseInflow | " + syncRes;

		// Close any open PO Shortage tickets the just-synced stock now covers.
		// Its own try/catch: a resolve failure must not make a completed inflow
		// look failed, and the sync's own result is what matters here.
		poRes = "";
		try
		{
			poRes = thisapp.resolvePurchaseShortages().toString();
			info "runPurchaseInflow | shortages -> " + poRes;
		}
		catch (ePo)
		{
			poRes = "{\"errors\":[\"" + ePo.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"]}";
			info "runPurchaseInflow | resolvePurchaseShortages threw: " + ePo.toString();
		}

		// The sync's reply is passed straight through rather than summarised, so
		// the button and the webhook log see exactly what Execute would.
		res = "{\"ran\":true,\"result\":" + syncRes + ",\"shortages\":" + poRes + "}";
	}
	catch (e)
	{
		errTxt = "DELUGE: " + e.toString();
	}

	// ONE EXIT, AT THE BOTTOM. Deluge's "all paths return" check does not count
	// a return that lives inside try/catch, and a function whose every return
	// sits in the try fails to save with "Missing return statement" reported
	// against the first line of the body.
	if(res == "")
	{
		if(errTxt == "")
		{
			errTxt = "Nothing ran";
		}
		res = "{\"ran\":false,\"reason\":\"" + errTxt.replaceAll("\"","'").replaceAll("\r","").replaceAll("\n"," | ") + "\"}";
	}

	return res;
}
